Application ID: scenario_034

## Description

A large enterprise freelancer marketplace allows contractors and consultants to access projects on behalf of organizations. Because many enterprise users authenticate using organization-managed email accounts, the platform supports organization-assisted account recovery when users lose access to their primary credentials.

The recovery workflow is designed around organizational trust. A recovery request is created for a user account and is associated with the organization to which the user belongs at the time of the request. Organization administrators are then responsible for reviewing and approving recovery requests before account access can be restored.

To support changing contractor assignments, organization memberships are synchronized from external identity and workforce management systems. As a result, user accounts may legitimately move between organizations after recovery requests have already been created or approved.

The platform validates recovery requests during creation, validates organizational authority during approval, and validates recovery tokens during completion. However, recovery approval is bound to the organizational context that existed when the recovery request was originally created.

During recovery completion, the platform verifies that the request is approved and that the recovery token is valid, but it never verifies that the organization which approved the recovery request still matches the account's current organization.

An attacker can exploit this inconsistency by leveraging an approval that was issued under one organizational context and later completing recovery after the account has moved into a different organizational context. The approval remains valid even though the underlying organizational relationship has changed.

---


