Application ID: scenario_047

## Description

A cloud-native secrets management platform securely stores and distributes database credentials, API keys, and service tokens used by internal applications. Every secret is represented by a canonical `SecretRecord` containing a unique secret identifier, version number, secret value, owner information, lifecycle state, and audit metadata. Secret rotation creates new versions while revoking previous ones, allowing applications to always retrieve the latest approved secret without directly interacting with the underlying storage.

Applications never access the secret repository directly. Instead, secret retrieval requests pass through a projection layer that converts canonical secret records into lightweight operational objects before validation and authorization are performed. Downstream components—including secret validation, access policy enforcement, serialization, and application services—correctly trust the operational representation they receive and never reconstruct information removed during projection.

The vulnerability occurs during the construction of the operational secret representation. While creating an `OperationalSecret`, the projection layer silently omits the security-critical `version` attribute even though it has been validated and preserved throughout storage and rotation workflows. As a result, downstream components can determine that a secret is active and authorized but can no longer distinguish which version of the secret is being used.

When secrets are rotated, previous versions are correctly revoked and new versions are correctly stored. However, because operational secrets no longer preserve version identity, applications may continue using an older revoked secret while every validation stage succeeds. The authorization workflow therefore remains correct, but the security boundary established by secret versioning is unintentionally lost during projection.

