Application ID: scenario_088

## Description

The platform is an industrial digital twin SaaS that lets manufacturing engineers monitor production lines, PLCs, robots, sensors, conveyors, and motors across one or more factories. Engineers use a dashboard to build ad-hoc telemetry searches, combining a factory, a time window, and a set of comparison filters on fields such as temperature, pressure, vibration, current, and voltage.

A search request first reaches a Go API gateway. The gateway authenticates the caller with a signed session token and rejects any request whose token is missing, expired, or improperly signed, and it also rejects any request whose role does not belong to the engineer or admin group. Once a caller is authenticated, the gateway checks that the requested factory and time range are present, and that every filter in the request uses one of a small set of approved field names and one of a small set of approved comparison operators (equals, greater-than, greater-than-or-equal, less-than, less-than-or-equal, and a between-two-values operator). A request that fails any of these checks is rejected before it goes any further.

Requests that pass the gateway's checks are handed to an internal query-builder service, written in a different part of the stack, whose job is to translate the approved field/operator/value combinations into a database query and run it. This internal service assumes that by the time a request reaches it, the gateway has already confirmed the request is well-formed, so it focuses only on building an efficient query rather than re-checking what has supposedly already been checked.

The trust failure sits at the boundary between those two layers. The gateway's approval only ever looks at the *names* of the field and the operator the caller supplied — it never looks at the *shape* of the comparison value itself, on the assumption that an approved operator name implies a simple, scalar comparison value. The query-builder service, for its part, contains a convenience shortcut meant to let previously-built, already-structured query pieces be reused as-is rather than rebuilt from scratch. That shortcut decides whether to reuse a value verbatim purely by inspecting the value's own shape, with no way to tell whether the shape came from the platform's own internal machinery or from a caller's request body. When a caller submits a comparison value that happens to have that reusable shape, the query-builder service passes it straight through to the database exactly as submitted, regardless of which operator name the gateway approved for it.

The practical effect is that an authenticated engineer, who is only supposed to be able to search their own factory's readings with simple numeric or equality comparisons, can submit a specially shaped comparison value that the database interprets as a much broader condition than any approved operator would produce — one that can match records regardless of factory, or regardless of the field's actual contents. The factory-scoping and field-comparison model the dashboard is built around quietly stops applying, and the search returns telemetry the caller was never meant to see.


