============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_086

## Description

The platform manages production infrastructure changes for large enterprises operating across multiple business units. Every production deployment must obtain three independent approvals before execution is permitted.

The approval workflow intentionally separates responsibility across different trust domains:

- Security Approval
- Compliance Approval
- Operations Approval

Each approval is issued independently by a dedicated approval authority and contains the approval category, approver identity, approval timestamp, and deployment identifier. No approval authority communicates directly with another.

External approvals first pass through protocol adapters that normalize incoming approval identities into a canonical internal representation before they are stored by the Approval Repository.

During deployment preparation, the Approval Collector retrieves every approval associated with the deployment request and constructs an immutable Deployment Plan containing the complete approval set. The Deployment Gate later evaluates the Deployment Plan and determines whether the deployment satisfies the enterprise approval policy before authorizing execution.

The trust boundary fails across three independent components.

During normalization, the Security Approval Adapter converts externally supplied approval identities into a canonical representation. Different external identity formats may therefore collapse into the same normalized approval identity.

Later, the Approval Collector deduplicates approvals using only the normalized approval identity instead of preserving both the approval category and approval identity. Distinct approvals that now appear identical silently overwrite one another during collection.

Finally, the Deployment Gate assumes the collected approval set already guarantees category uniqueness. Instead of verifying that Security, Compliance, and Operations approvals are each independently present, it authorizes deployments whenever the number of unique collected approvals satisfies the required quorum.

Each individual behavior is operationally reasonable. Identity normalization simplifies interoperability with external approval systems. Approval deduplication removes duplicate approval submissions. Quorum evaluation assumes earlier workflow stages have already enforced approval uniqueness.

Individually, none of these behaviors violate the security policy. Only when all three weaknesses occur together does the platform lose the ability to distinguish independent approval categories while continuing to satisfy the deployment quorum.

Deployment execution, rollout coordination, audit logging, authentication, repository integrity, and deployment orchestration all execute correctly. The platform ultimately authorizes a production deployment without obtaining every required independent approval category, violating the enterprise approval policy while preserving operational correctness.


