============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_082

## Description

Large enterprises continuously rotate secrets consumed by Kubernetes,
CI/CD pipelines, service mesh, internal APIs, and database clusters. A
secret is never installed directly after approval. Instead, every rotation
passes through several independent operational stages: a rotation request
is approved by security, the approved rotation is published, a
synchronization planner assembles a package for the target provider, the
provider synchronizes the secret into its own store, and a runtime
installer writes it into the workload's secret store.

Every approved rotation is uniquely identified by the combination of its
secret identifier, its rotation epoch, and its secret version. Downstream
stages are expected to preserve or re-resolve all three fields so that
whatever eventually reaches a running workload is provably the exact
secret generation that security approved, not merely a secret that shares
its identifier.

The approval service works correctly: it computes rotation epochs, assigns
secret versions, encrypts secrets, signs approvals, and can always
reconstruct an exact approved rotation from its canonical identity. The
publication, synchronization, and provider stages each expose both a
canonical, identity-preserving lookup and a convenience lookup meant for
dashboards and health tooling. Each stage independently chose the
convenience path for its primary workflow, reasoning that publish-then-sync
happens quickly enough that "newest" and "the one just approved" are
almost always the same secret. Individually, each shortcut is masked by a
downstream stage that still performs a canonical check. Only when a second
rotation for the same secret is approved and pushed through publication
before the first rotation's package finishes synchronizing does the
pipeline silently install a newer, unapproved secret generation instead of
the one that was actually security-approved.


