============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_043

## Description

A hospital Picture Archiving and Communication System (PACS) manages radiology studies originating from multiple hospitals within a healthcare network. Incoming DICOM studies are validated, normalized, cataloged, and transformed into search records so radiologists can efficiently locate imaging studies across the network.

To maintain separation between imaging ingestion and search infrastructure, the system converts catalog metadata into a lightweight search model that is consumed by indexing, authorization, search planning, and radiology search services. Every downstream component assumes this search model accurately represents the original study metadata established during DICOM ingestion.

The vulnerability occurs during construction of the search record. While building the operational search representation, a security-critical authorization attribute identifying the study's hospital partition is not propagated into the final search model. All downstream components—including indexing, authorization, policy enforcement, and radiology search—correctly operate on the search record they receive, but that record no longer faithfully represents the original study.

As a result, authorization decisions are performed against an incomplete representation of study ownership, allowing search results from hospital partitions outside the user's authorized scope to be returned even though every downstream security component behaves according to its intended responsibility.

