============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_081

## Description

Large enterprises do not deploy approved container images directly. Instead,
every release passes through a chain of independent operational stages
before it reaches a production cluster: a developer build is approved by a
security review, the approved release is published, distribution assembles
a package for rollout, a deployment stage selects the package to ship, and a
runtime executor deploys it into the Kubernetes cluster. Each stage
intentionally produces its own runtime artifact rather than passing the
original object through unchanged.

Every approved release is uniquely identified by the combination of its
release identifier, its artifact digest, and the approval generation under
which it was reviewed. Downstream stages are expected to preserve or
re-resolve all three fields so that whatever eventually gets deployed is
provably the exact artifact that security approved, not merely an artifact
that shares its name.

The approval service works correctly: it computes digests, assigns approval
generations, signs releases, and can always reconstruct an exact approved
release from its canonical identity. The publication, distribution, and
deployment stages each expose both a canonical, identity-preserving lookup
and a convenience lookup meant for dashboards and operational tooling. Each
stage independently chose the convenience path for its primary workflow,
reasoning that publish-then-promote happens quickly enough that "newest"
and "the one just approved" are almost always the same thing. Individually,
each shortcut is masked by a downstream stage that still performs a
canonical check. Only when a release train pushes more than one build in
close succession, and all three shortcuts line up back to back, does the
pipeline silently promote a newer, unapproved container image instead of
the one that was actually security-approved.


