============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_076

## Description

A large enterprise operates an AI Agent Execution Platform that enables autonomous agents to perform approved enterprise operations across internal systems. Rather than allowing language models to invoke enterprise tools directly, every tool execution must first pass through a centralized approval workflow.

When an agent produces a Tool Invocation Plan, the Tool Approval Service validates the requested operation and constructs an immutable **ApprovedToolInvocation** that binds the approved execution to a specific enterprise endpoint. This approval permanently records the exact execution context using the approved **toolId**, **endpointId**, and **approvalGeneration**, together with the approved arguments, timeout, and approval timestamp.

Before execution begins, the approved invocation is projected into a runtime execution object consumed by the execution gateway. The runtime object is intentionally isolated from the planning and approval pipeline so that execution components never communicate with approval services or reconstruct approval decisions during execution.

The execution gateway receives the runtime invocation, resolves the corresponding enterprise connector, and dispatches execution through the enterprise MCP infrastructure to systems such as Kubernetes, Jira, Slack, ServiceNow, SQL reporting services, and internal APIs.

The platform guarantees that every runtime execution preserves the exact Approved Tool Execution Binding created during approval. Runtime execution must therefore continue using the same approved endpoint and approval generation throughout the execution pipeline, ensuring that tool execution always corresponds to the operation originally reviewed and approved.

This guarantee is violated during runtime projection.

While converting the immutable **ApprovedToolInvocation** into **RuntimeToolInvocation**, the projection process correctly preserves the approved tool identifier, arguments, timeout, and approval timestamp, but accidentally discards the security-critical **endpointId** and **approvalGeneration** fields. The runtime execution artifact therefore no longer uniquely identifies the execution approval originally issued by the approval service.

Because execution components intentionally trust the runtime object and never reconstruct approval information, the execution gateway resolves the current active endpoint associated with the requested tool. If endpoint assignments have changed since approval, execution proceeds against a different endpoint even though every approval, authentication, authorization, connector validation, and execution step succeeds normally.

The vulnerability therefore violates execution approval continuity without bypassing authentication, authorization, or approval, requiring architectural reasoning across multiple components to identify the loss of the approved execution binding.

---

## Normal Flow

An enterprise user submits an agent request.

The LLM Planner generates a Tool Invocation Plan.

The Tool Approval Service validates the requested operation.

An immutable ApprovedToolInvocation is created.

InvocationProjectionService constructs a RuntimeToolInvocation.

ToolExecutionGateway dispatches execution.

The MCP Connector invokes the approved enterprise tool.

AuditLogger records the completed execution.

---


