============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_025

## Description

A warehouse robotics platform is used to control autonomous forklifts, conveyor robots, and inventory transport robots operating inside large fulfillment centers. Operators interact with the platform through a centralized API which dispatches commands to robots through a messaging and gateway infrastructure.

The platform supports both operational commands and safety-critical commands. Operational commands include actions such as moving inventory, picking items, and starting conveyor systems. Safety-critical commands include disabling collision detection, disabling speed limiters, and enabling safety override mode. These commands can significantly alter robot behavior and therefore require supervisor authorization.

The command execution pipeline spans multiple technologies and trust boundaries. API requests are accepted through CommandController.go and validated by AuthorizationService.go. Authorized commands are packaged by CommandDispatchService.go, serialized by Rust messaging services, translated by a C++ gateway layer, and ultimately executed by embedded C firmware running on robots.

The system appears secure because supervisor authorization is correctly enforced during command submission. AuthorizationService.go verifies that safety-critical commands may only be issued by users with supervisor privileges. Authorized commands also contain an AuthorizationContext object containing user identity, role information, and request source metadata.

However, the authorization context is not preserved throughout the command lifecycle. During command translation, the gateway converts transport-layer messages into firmware commands and forwards only operator identity information while discarding authorization metadata. As a result, robot firmware receives command type information and operator identifiers but never receives the role information required to verify whether a command was originally authorized for execution.

The firmware trust model assumes that all commands arriving from the gateway have already been validated. SafetyController.c therefore executes safety-critical commands without independently verifying supervisor authorization. Because the authorization context was discarded before crossing the trust boundary into the robotics network, safety-critical commands can ultimately be executed without end-to-end authorization validation.

The vulnerability is not caused by incorrect authorization policies, role assignments, safety policies, message serialization, robot capability definitions, or audit logging. The root cause is that authorization context is lost during command translation, preventing downstream components from enforcing the security guarantees established at the API layer.


