============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_041

## Description

The Cloud Cluster Manager coordinates distributed shard migrations across database clusters. Before traffic can be redirected from one shard to another, participating nodes execute a consensus protocol to ensure that a quorum has agreed to the migration. Once consensus completes successfully, a `ConsensusCommit` and a corresponding `CommitCertificate` are created as authoritative proof that the migration is safe to execute.

The migration workflow then projects the completed consensus state into a `MigrationPlan`, which becomes the object consumed by downstream validation, authorization, and migration services. Security controls throughout the pipeline validate cluster information, leader terms, commit indices, migration topology, and authorization policy before the migration coordinator initiates the traffic switch.

Despite these protections, the transition between the consensus stage and the migration stage incorrectly assumes that consensus proof no longer needs to be represented after validation. During projection, the migration plan preserves operational routing information but silently discards the consensus confirmation state. Downstream services correctly validate and authorize the migration plan they receive but no longer possess cryptographic evidence that distributed consensus actually completed.

Because execution is ultimately authorized using an object that no longer carries complete consensus state, traffic can be switched based on an incomplete representation of the original distributed agreement, violating the integrity of the consensus workflow.

