Application ID: scenario_029

## Description

A pharmaceutical clinical trial management platform coordinates the lifecycle of trial protocols, ethics approvals, patient enrollment, and trial execution. Before patients may be enrolled into a clinical trial, the protocol must receive approval from an ethics review board. Approvals are version-specific and are intended to apply only to the exact protocol revision that was reviewed.

Researchers may modify trial protocols after approval. Regulatory requirements mandate that any material protocol modification automatically invalidates previous approvals and requires a new review cycle before enrollment can continue. The platform correctly implements approval invalidation and maintains an authoritative approval repository that tracks current approval status.

To support asynchronous workflows and reduce load on approval systems, approved protocols are periodically transformed into approval snapshots. These snapshots are distributed to downstream workflow components through a messaging infrastructure and stored in a snapshot repository. Snapshots represent approval state at the moment they are created and are intended for workflow optimization rather than authorization decisions.

When a protocol is modified, the authoritative approval repository correctly revokes the previous approval. However, previously generated approval snapshots remain available in the snapshot repository. A downstream enrollment workflow uses these historical snapshots to determine whether patient enrollment is permitted instead of consulting the authoritative approval repository.

As a result, enrollment decisions may be made using outdated approval information. A protocol can be modified after approval, the approval can be revoked correctly, and yet patient enrollment may still proceed because the enrollment workflow trusts an old snapshot that indicates the protocol was approved at an earlier point in time.

The vulnerability is not caused by missing approval invalidation, broken versioning, missing approval metadata, event synchronization failures, controller authorization flaws, or repository corruption. The root cause is the use of historical approval snapshots as an authorization source instead of the current authoritative approval state.

