============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_099

## Description

The system is an enterprise secret distribution fabric responsible for securely delivering short-lived credentials to thousands of workloads executing across production, staging, and development environments. No single service owns the final runtime secret. Instead, every runtime credential is assembled immediately before workload deployment from several independently managed infrastructure services, each of which contributes one stage to the runtime secret lease pipeline: a lease service, a rotation service, a cluster binding service, a synchronization service, and a runtime materialization service.

The normal workflow begins when a workload submits a `SecretAccessRequest` to `SecretApprovalAuthority`, which validates the request and issues an `ApprovedSecretLease`. Every approved lease carries a canonical lease identity consisting of `secretId`, `clusterId`, `workloadScope`, `rotationGeneration`, and `leaseEpoch`, together with workload identity, granted permissions, approval metadata, lease duration, and authorization information. This canonical identity uniquely represents one authorized runtime secret lease and is intended to remain unchanged throughout every downstream transformation.

Following approval, multiple infrastructure services begin constructing independent runtime artifacts. `LeaseProjector` projects the approved lease into a `RuntimeLease`. `RotationResolver` independently resolves the currently active rotation into a `RotationLease`. The cluster service correctly constructs a `ClusterBinding` representing deployment-specific workload placement. None of these services owns the complete lease authorization; each operates only on the subset of lease information relevant to its own infrastructure domain.

A `LeaseSynchronizationAssembler` later receives all three lease components and combines them into a `RuntimeSecretLease`. That synchronized lease is subsequently consumed by `RuntimeSecretMaterializer`, which constructs a deployment-ready `MaterializedRuntimeSecret`. Finally, `SecretDeliveryGateway` distributes the resulting runtime secret throughout enterprise workloads. Workload consumers intentionally trust the supplied MaterializedRuntimeSecret and never reconstruct lease authorization during secret delivery.

`SecretApprovalAuthority` correctly validates requests, issues approvals, maintains lease generations, and guarantees that every ApprovedSecretLease accurately represents one authorized runtime credential. The individual runtime artifacts are also structurally valid. Runtime leases correctly represent approved lease metadata, rotation leases correctly represent active secret rotations, cluster bindings accurately describe workload placement, synchronized runtime leases satisfy every local validation rule, and materialized runtime secrets are internally consistent. The weakness does not exist in any individual lease artifact or any individual infrastructure component. Instead, it emerges because multiple services independently conclude that preserving only a subset of the canonical lease identity is sufficient for their local processing, while downstream services assume every upstream transformation has already preserved complete lease continuity.

Each shortcut appears individually reasonable. Lease projection assumes workload scope can be reconstructed from deployment metadata and therefore need not be propagated explicitly. Rotation resolution assumes only one active lease exists for a given secret and therefore caches resolved leases using the secret identifier alone. Lease synchronization assumes upstream services have already validated lease epochs and therefore verifies only structural compatibility between independently generated lease artifacts. Runtime materialization assumes runtime secrets should be aggressively reused for workloads consuming the same enterprise secret and therefore derives cache identities from a stable secret identifier instead of the complete canonical lease identity. None of these assumptions independently produces an exploitable runtime secret. Combined, however, they allow lease artifacts originating from different workloads, different secret rotations, and different lease epochs to converge into one RuntimeSecretLease and ultimately one MaterializedRuntimeSecret that no longer faithfully represents any single ApprovedSecretLease.

The attacker's objective is therefore to obtain a materialized runtime secret whose workload scope, rotation generation, or lease epoch no longer collectively correspond to the same ApprovedSecretLease, without forging approvals, bypassing the approval authority, compromising any individual infrastructure service, or directly modifying secret delivery.


