============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_031

## Description

An industrial drone management platform distributes over-the-air (OTA) firmware updates to fleets of autonomous drones operating in logistics, surveying, and infrastructure inspection environments. To prevent unauthorized firmware from reaching production fleets, firmware releases must pass a release approval workflow before deployment.

Each firmware package uploaded to the platform is assigned a SHA-256 hash that uniquely identifies the binary artifact. Release engineers review firmware packages and generate signed release manifests describing the exact firmware artifact approved for deployment. A release manifest contains metadata such as firmware version, target drone model, signer identity, and the approved firmware hash.

Before deploying a firmware update campaign, the platform validates that a release manifest exists, that the manifest was approved by a trusted signer, that the firmware version matches the manifest, that the target drone model matches the manifest, and that the deployment campaign is active. Once validation succeeds, deployment jobs are created and distributed to drone fleets.

The vulnerability arises because the deployment workflow validates manifest trust but never verifies that the firmware artifact being deployed is the same firmware artifact approved by the signed manifest. The deployment service loads both the firmware package and the release manifest but compares only version and device compatibility information. The firmware package SHA-256 hash is never compared against the approved firmware hash contained in the manifest.

As a result, an attacker can upload a malicious firmware package that uses the same firmware version and target model as an approved release. By referencing a legitimate signed manifest and a different firmware package during deployment, the attacker can cause the system to deploy firmware that was never approved by the release process.

The vulnerability is not caused by broken signature validation, incorrect hash generation, repository corruption, deployment worker behavior, rollout policy failures, campaign authorization issues, or firmware upload flaws. The root cause is the absence of manifest-to-artifact binding validation during deployment authorization.

